{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook and the turbulent relationships that shaped its rise. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is not just a biopic—it’s a sharp, fast-paced drama about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing his brilliance, social awkwardness, and emotional detachment with uncanny precision. his portrayal is cold yet compelling, making zuckerberg an enigma you can’t look away from.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally strong. andrew garfield shines as edward sneed, the loyal friend turned bitter rival, while jesse plemons brings quiet intensity to the role of justin tucker. the film’s legal framework, structured as two parallel depositions, gives the narrative a gripping rhythm that never drags. sorkin’s dialogue is razor-sharp, witty, and layered with subtext—every line feels deliberate, every pause loaded with meaning.\n\njames newton howard’s haunting score, dominated by a pulsing, electronic motif, perfectly mirrors the cold, relentless energy of the tech world. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is sleek and modern, using shadows and tight framing to emphasize isolation and tension. even the editing, by danny cohen, is a character in itself—rapid, precise, and unrelenting, echoing the speed of the internet age.\n\nwhat makes the film truly exceptional is how it avoids simple hero-villain dynamics. zuckerberg isn’t portrayed as a villain, nor are his accusers portrayed as purely noble. instead, the movie explores the ambiguity of success: how innovation can be born from loneliness, how friendships can crumble under ambition, and how the pursuit of connection can lead to profound disconnection.\n\nthe social network isn’t just about facebook—it’s about the new rules of power in the 21st century, where code replaces charisma, and algorithms dictate influence. it’s a chilling, brilliant, and timely portrait of a generation that built the future without understanding the human cost. by the end, you’re left not with answers, but with questions—about friendship, ownership, and what we sacrifice when we connect the world.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.3307040333747864}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0008434075862169266}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.004730462562292814}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -8.165503095369786e-05}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.078758955001831}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.005942178890109062}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.5509715676307678}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00012289722508285195}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00023123928986024112}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.35386478900909424}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -6.556489552167477e-06}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -0.9126428365707397}, {"token": " relationships", "logprob": -0.6572774052619934}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.6558628082275391}, {"token": " shaped", "logprob": -0.836301326751709}, {"token": " its", "logprob": -0.0012380562257021666}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.904449462890625}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.00022516099852509797}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.00018380382971372455}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.0007051127031445503}, {"token"